{
  "result": "I'd make it so that changing your mind doesn't feel like losing. Right now, admitting you were wrong often costs people status, friendships, or their sense of who they are, so they dig in, defend old positions, and dismiss evidence. A lot of the world's stubborn problems, from bad policy to family feuds to scientists clinging to failed theories, persist for that reason.\n\nIf being wrong were cheap, people could update quickly when new evidence arrived, argue in good faith, and say \"I was mistaken\" without it being a humiliation. Disagreements would more often get resolved instead of entrenched. It wouldn't fix everything, since people still disagree about values and have unequal access to information, but I think it would unlock a lot of progress.\n\nWhat would you change?",
